# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is deliberately NO SPA catch-all rule below. Do not add one.
#
# SPA fallback is handled by Cloudflare Pages' built-in behaviour instead:
# when the output directory contains no 404.html, Pages serves index.html
# with a 200 for any path that doesn't match a static asset. That is why
# public/404.html was deleted — see scripts/check-redirects.mjs, which fails
# the build if either the catch-all or the 404.html comes back.
#
# Every catch-all shape was measured against the real Cloudflare rule engine
# (`wrangler pages dev`, wrangler 4.118) on 2026-08-01. All of them are wrong:
#
#   /*  /index.html  200   Wrangler drops the rule outright — "Infinite loop
#                          detected in this rule and has been ignored": Pages
#                          strips `.html`/`/index` from the target, which
#                          re-enters `/*`. With the rule gone, every unmatched
#                          path fell through to 404.html, so /help, /city/:slug
#                          and every sitemap 404'd site-wide. The warning is
#                          printed on every deploy and was never read.
#
#   /*  /  200             Parses, but a 200-rewrite outranks static-asset
#                          serving, so /robots.txt, /build-id.txt AND every
#                          hashed /assets/**.{js,css} are served the SPA HTML.
#                          Strict MIME checking then rejects the stylesheet and
#                          the module scripts. (#2469, reverted in #2473.)
#
#   /section/*  /index.html  200   Same infinite-loop drop as `/*`.
#   /section    /index.html  200   Parses, then 308-redirects to `/` — because
#                          Pages canonicalises /index.html -> / at the asset
#                          layer. No rewrite whose target is /index.html can
#                          ever work here.
#
# Status 404 is likewise not expressible: Pages accepts only 200, 301, 302,
# 303, 307 and 308 in this file. The four `/assets/* /404.html 404` guards
# that used to live here were rejected by the parser on every deploy and
# never did anything.
#
# The built-in fallback answers *any* unmatched path with index.html,
# hashed-asset URLs included — so on its own a stale bundle asking for a
# deleted chunk would get 200 text/html rather than a 404. functions/
# _middleware.ts closes that: it inspects asset-like paths and turns an HTML
# answer into a real 404. That only works while Pages Functions are routed,
# which is what public/_routes.json guarantees — do not delete it.
# public/sw.js does the same client-side as a second layer, and
# scripts/smoke-pages.sh asserts all of it after every deploy.
#
# DO NOT add identity rewrites like `/sw.js /sw.js 200` — they create
# infinite rewrite loops that abort rule parsing for the whole file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
